    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's what I implemented and why it keeps structural impact low.\n\n## Change summary\n\n**The rule** \u2014 at `POST /api/owners`, assign `customerCode = <LAST3>-<NNNN>`: upper-cased first three letters of `lastName`, and a global 4-digit sequence equal to `(current owner count) + 1`. It's fixed at creation and persisted, so `GET` returns it back.\n\n**Files touched (6):**\n\n1. **`AssignCustomerCode.java`** (new) \u2014 one small function class, CC\u22481, zero surrounding class complexity. It reads `lastName`, computes the code, mutates the `@Val Owner` in place:\n   ```java\n   int sequence = ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1;\n   owner.setCustomerCode(String.format(\"%s-%04d\", last3, sequence));\n   ```\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `assignCustomerCode` step between `build` and `save`, inside the same transaction governance. No existing step changed logic.\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added one persisted field plus a plain getter/setter (each CC 1). No existing method edited.\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `customerCode` as a `readOnly` property on the `Owner` schema. This regenerates `OwnerDto` with the field, and MapStruct auto-maps it by name \u2014 so **`OwnerMapper` needed no change**.\n5. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `customer_code VARCHAR(20)` column.\n6. **`db/hsqldb/data.sql`** \u2014 appended a `NULL` to the 10 seed inserts (they use column-less `VALUES`, so the count had to match).\n\n**Why this shape:** I put the new behaviour in its own cohesive unit rather than growing `BuildOwner` or `SaveOwner` (which carry more context), avoided the mapper entirely by letting name-based mapping do the work, and computed the sequence via the existing `findAll()` so the `OwnerRepository` interface and its three implementations stayed untouched.\n\nFull suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS` (the repo currently contains only the acceptance test).\n\nOne note for later: the sequence is `count + 1` at creation, matching the spec exactly. With soft-deletes retained in the table, the count still increments monotonically between consecutive creates, so the \"+1\" relationship the spec requires holds \u2014 but it isn't collision-proof across a delete-then-create if you ever add a uniqueness constraint. The spec defines it as a formula, not a unique key, so I left it as specified.",
